@guylepage3 It depends. The idea for this was because it makes more sense for the default to go the content that changes, which is [Pages](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/pages-and-layouts#pages), not the [Layout](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/pages-and-layouts#pages) itself (which only renders once). We wouldn't want to scroll to the top of a page if the content that's changing is outside the viewport.

> Another solution could be to set allow devs to set the scroll position

Nevertheless, I definitely think we should still offer a way to have the behavior scroll to the top of the page! I mentioned this as well in an related issue → https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/59387#issuecomment-1987393451.

@guylepage3 I've tested this extensively across multiple reported <picture data-single-emoji=":github2:" title=":github2:"><img class="emoji" width="20" height="auto" src="https://emoji.slack-edge.com/T0CAQ00TU/github2/1e0df3cb125c556e.png" alt=":github2:" align="absmiddle"></picture> issues (I closed out several, and the one you've recently commented out I will doing so as well) of folks reporting the behavior (to your point, it wasn't expected for them), and I haven't been able to find a 🐛 in the expected behavior.

I'll circle back with our team to discuss about how we could provide a way to opt out of this behavior in the meantime.

If you can provide a <picture data-single-emoji=":repro:" title=":repro:"><img class="emoji" width="20" height="auto" src="https://emoji.slack-edge.com/T0CAQ00TU/repro/5cea53b5532825b4.png" alt=":repro:" align="absmiddle"></picture> that showcases the behavior breaking, I'll take a look at that as well!

@samcx It seems as though almost every Next app I come across has this issue. Reminder, that this only appears on Google Chrome on iOS. I thought it might have been the chrome bug shift but that seems to not be the case either. Super strange. 

